# Test: the reframe moved the frame and none of the payload
#
# Flow:
# 1. A native Go fixture writes a config declaring one external Go SDK plugin
# 2. The daemon starts, spawns `ze-test record-plugin`, and opens SSH
# 3. `ze init` creates operator credentials
# 4. `show test records fault | raw` reads the COLLAPSED wire shape
# 5. `show test records walk | raw` reads the STREAMED wire shape
# 6. Every row of both is compared, byte for byte, with the bytes its producer
#    writes
#
# The producer is recordRow.AppendTo (internal/test/cli/cmd_record_plugin.go).
# It appends `{"index":<n>,"fill":"<fill>"}` with no marshaler, and this spec
# changed no byte of it: `git diff c08252e0a^ HEAD --
# internal/test/cli/cmd_record_plugin.go` touches comments, two renamed item
# type constants and one new command, and no line of AppendTo or recordRows. So
# the literal this test builds IS the byte string an operator received before
# the reframe started, and the comparison is against that rather than against a
# second reading of the same wire.
#
# THE POINT. Every other answer test reads the frame: the kinds, the counted
# fields, the terminator. A frame that parsed can still deliver a payload the
# producer did not write, and no test here would have said so. This one asserts
# the payload and nothing else, so it goes red on a changed payload byte and
# stays green on a changed frame. That is the whole claim of this spec stated as
# a test: only the frame moved.
#
# Both wire shapes are read because the payload takes a different road through
# each. A walk that ends inside rpc.AnswerBufferThreshold collapses into ONE
# counted record carrying the whole document, and a walk that passes it writes
# one counted record for each row. `show test records fault` is the first, at
# twelve rows, and `show test records walk` is the second, at three hundred. A
# byte lost in either road would be lost in only one, and the row count is what
# chooses the road (WriteRecordAnswer, pkg/plugin/rpc/answer_write.go).
#
# `| raw` is the rendering both use, and `| ndjson` cannot serve here. It decodes
# each record and re-encodes it so that the same answer reads the same however
# many records it holds, which alphabetizes the producer's key order
# (writeRecordJSON, internal/component/command/render_records.go). That is a
# rendering the operator asked for, and this test is about the bytes underneath
# it. plugin-owned-command-streams.ci owns the `| ndjson` reading.
#
# The fault walk also carries a row wider than one wire message, which has no
# line at all. What that row's rejection states is frame arithmetic
# (encoded-bytes moves with the prefix width), so this test reads its shape and
# never its numbers. plugin-command-partial-fault.ci owns that assertion.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-8 -- the payload bytes an operator receives are the bytes the
# command produced, unchanged by the reframe.
# VALIDATES: R-6's mitigation -- a payload change is visible rather than hidden
# under the fixture churn the reframe cost.
# PREVENTS: a frame that parses cleanly and hands the operator data the producer
# never wrote: a count clamped to what arrived, a payload byte rewritten on its
# way to the wire, or a row re-marshaled between the plugin and the operator.

# Native fixture: two wire shapes, one producer, byte for byte

# Process orchestration
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/answer-payload-unchanged:timeout=90s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: [0-9]+ collapsed rows are the bytes the producer wrote
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: [0-9]+ streamed rows are the bytes the producer wrote
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
